Actual metadata captured during the current run (only these two environment keys
were selected; no complete environment was dumped):
  Automated editor PID9367, net:[4026531840]:
    WEBKIT_INSPECTOR_SERVER=127.0.0.1:37711
    TAURI_WEBVIEW_AUTOMATION=true
  Supervised production child PID9437, net:[4026532264]:
    WEBKIT_INSPECTOR_SERVER absent
    TAURI_WEBVIEW_AUTOMATION=false

The original automated editor8959 and WebProcess8991 had already exited before
inspection, so their exact environment was not captured. Their retained observer
record showed the WebProcess had Seccomp2/filter1, NoNewPrivs1, distinct mount/PID/
user namespaces, and the editor's same network namespace. The matching upstream
code plus current live inspector metadata explains this WebDriver-specific scope.
It does not establish network isolation for automated editor WebProcesses.

The unchanged strict observer correctly rejects that automated-editor network
scope. It must not be softened or reported as an all-namespaces pass. Production
children sanitize the inspector variable and disable automation; their actual
namespace observations are separate evidence. A current-package normal desktop
launch without automation must also be observed separately by the parent task.
The retained /tmp/pn-m3-current-preview-sandbox.log was read after completion:
its strict observer reports errors:[], passed:true and the PASS assertion for the
production preview identified by the parent as PID9437.

No UI, WebDriver, service, profile, environment, or sysctl mutation was performed
during this follow-up. Only /proc metadata and upstream source were read.
